@@ -90,7 +90,10 @@ The `context` object contains:
 - `attemptNumber` - The attempt number (starts at 1)
 - `retriesLeft` - Number of retries remaining
 - `retriesConsumed` - Number of retries consumed so far
-- `retryDelay` - The delay in milliseconds before the next retry (based on `minTimeout`, `factor`, `maxTimeout`, and `randomize`). The actual delay may be shorter if it would exceed `maxRetryTime`. This is `0` when no retry will occur based on the checks completed before the current callback runs. `shouldConsumeRetry` is the exception: it is shown the delay that would be used, since deciding whether to consume the retry is what it is being asked.
+- `retryDelay` - The delay in milliseconds before the next retry (based on `minTimeout`, `factor`, `maxTimeout`, and `randomize`, plus the error's `retryAfter` if it has one). The actual delay may be shorter if it would exceed `maxRetryTime`. This is `0` when no retry will occur based on the checks completed before the current callback runs. `shouldConsumeRetry` is the exception: it is shown the delay that would be used, since deciding whether to consume the retry is what it is being asked.
+- `elapsedTime` - The time in milliseconds since `pRetry` was called, measured when the callback is invoked
+
+If the error has a numeric `retryAfter` (in seconds), for example from a `Retry-After` header, it is waited out before the next attempt, **in addition to** the normal backoff delay. The backoff is kept so that many clients given the same `retryAfter` do not all return at the same moment. `maxTimeout` caps the backoff only, and `maxRetryTime` still bounds the total. A failure that does not consume a retry has no backoff, so only the `retryAfter` is waited. Values that are not finite and positive are ignored.
 
